/* kernel/crypto/keccak_kat.h — PINNED FIPS 202 known-answer vectors, DDR-1052.
 *
 * GENERATED, NOT HAND-WRITTEN, and generated by an INDEPENDENT implementation:
 * Python's hashlib (SHA-3/SHAKE), which is not this code. That is the whole
 * point. A gate that hashes something and then checks the hash against ITSELF
 * -- or that signs and then verifies -- passes on any self-consistent wrong
 * implementation, which is the dead-arm class this project has now hit nine
 * times. Conformance can only be shown against fixed expected values produced
 * somewhere else.
 *
 * CROSS-CHECKED against the published FIPS 202 constants: SHAKE128("") begins
 * 7F9C2BA4, SHAKE256("") begins 46B9DD2B, SHA3-256("") is A7FFC6F8... -- so
 * hashlib itself is corroborated by public knowledge rather than merely trusted.
 *
 * WHY THESE LENGTHS. The empty and "abc" cases are the standard smoke vectors
 * and catch nothing subtle. The load-bearing ones are the RATE BOUNDARIES:
 * SHAKE128 absorbs 168 bytes per permutation and SHAKE256 absorbs 136, so
 * inputs of rate-1 / rate / rate+1 are where pad10*1 and the multi-block absorb
 * go wrong -- an implementation that mishandles them still passes every short
 * vector. The two 200-byte tails cover the other direction: a SQUEEZE longer
 * than the rate, which needs a further permutation mid-output.
 *
 * The message for the patN cases is byte i = (7*i + 3) & 0xFF.
 */
